Check authority for the particular object and effect when it is used (@computing-trust-authority: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-input-validity-origin-authority-and-truth.lingua#r_00A3852C60830A63E0B4F976AA") { r_00250BA8CBCE73EF3045E82456
An identity names an actor; authentication supplies evidence for that identity; authorization determines permitted operations in a context. Neither a familiar display name nor a well-formed request grants access. Reuse [Define controlled services and distinguish errors from process faults](Define-controlled-services-and-distinguish-errors-from-process-faults.lingua#r_0006F078C2118768132F7C3690): trusted entry establishes a boundary that an ordinary procedure name alone does not enforce. A Python function parameter called actor is not an authentication mechanism.

Represent a permission as (actor, object, operation). In the original ledger wrapper, read permits obtaining the latest matching receipt; add permits requesting a new addition, provided read is also present because this endpoint returns a result. These choices define this particular service, not a universal access policy. The grant set is trusted administrative state. Requests carry only an operation identity and amount; a trusted caller supplies the authenticated actor and resolved object. Each object has its own existing ledger protocol and one client namespace. There is no new multi-client ordering protocol.

The decision and modeled effect occur in one atomic transition relative to grant changes. First validate the public request shape and ranges. If read is absent, return no receipt and no effect. If the request matches the latest retained identity and amount, return its receipt with no new addition. Otherwise require add and pass the request to the unchanged ledger transition, which rejects gaps, stale identities and changed arguments. A new result is disclosed only through that guarded transition. Revoking add prevents a later new addition; it does not undo a prior addition. Revoking read prevents a new disclosure by the service, but cannot erase an already delivered receipt or recall a response already in flight.

[Protection of Information in Computer Systems by Saltzer and Schroeder](../sources/Protection-of-Information-in-Computer-Systems-by-Saltzer-and-Schroeder.lingua#r_0089CCD9AFEBC6BD19EFFB6851) motivates this original reconstruction of stale permission. At time t₀ a draft is prepared with add permission. At t₁ that permission is revoked. At t₂ submission arrives. Using the t₀ grant snapshot permits an effect the current policy forbids. Checking current grants within the atomic effect step rejects it. If checking and committing are split across concurrent steps, a revocation can occur between them; a real implementation needs synchronization or a carefully specified versioned authorization mechanism. The model assumes that atomic boundary rather than implementing it.

**Work:** trace revocation before commit, after commit but before reply, and after confirmation. State separately the remaining effect and the available evidence. Explain why a read-only reviewer can recover an existing matching receipt but cannot append. Give an alpha grant and a beta request that must not transfer authority across objects. Identify the trusted components and prove by induction that every new modeled effect had both required grants at its transition. Do not extend this proof to malicious Python callers, transport authentication, side channels or real deployment.

**Continue:** [Separate input validity origin authority and truth](Separate-input-validity-origin-authority-and-truth.lingua#r_00A3852C60830A63E0B4F976AA).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
